      "type": "thematicExploration",
      "text": "At its core, The Maltese Falcon (1941) explores the corrupting power of greed and the illusion of honor among thieves. The titular falcon serves as a perfect MacGuffin, driving the plot while symbolizing the futility of material pursuit. Spade's adherence to his own moral code, despite its cynical nature, provides the only anchor in a world where truth is as shadowy as the film's cinematography."
    },
    {
